# Mutation table for PR #3058's plugin half: the config-PUT sink that swaps the
# ladder into the live engine and QUEUES the re-band (D4/D5).
#
# Format: NAME | FILE | PERL_EXPRESSION      (see scripts/mutation-matrix.sh)
# Run:
#   scripts/mutation-matrix.sh \
#     --pkg ./internal/plugins/dedup/ \
#     --table scripts/mutation-tables/dedup-score-config-plugin.muts
#
# WHAT THIS TABLE IS FOR. Every failure this guards against returns a real
# operation id to the caller and looks like a success. M01 is the sharpest:
# Registry.EnqueueOp collapses an enqueue onto an ACTIVE op with the same
# ConcurrencyKey and BYTE-EQUAL params, so without a per-ladder discriminator a
# second config PUT arriving mid-re-band is handed the RUNNING op's id and
# queues nothing — and that op read its ladder at start, so it finishes the
# whole backlog under the OLD ladder while the PUT reports success.

M01 drop the per-ladder discriminator so two ladders queue one re-band | internal/plugins/dedup/rescore_op.go | s/\t\tLadderFingerprint: ladderFingerprint\(cfg\),\n//

M02 apply the ladder to the live engine but never queue the re-band | internal/plugins/dedup/rescore_op.go | s/\topID, err := p\.registry\.EnqueueOp\(ctx, "dedup\.rescore", RescoreParams\{/\treturn "", nil\n\topID, err := p.registry.EnqueueOp(ctx, "dedup.rescore", RescoreParams{/

M03 give the re-band its own key so it can race a full scan | internal/plugins/dedup/rescore_op.go | s/\t\tConcurrencyKey: "dedup\.full-scan",/\t\tConcurrencyKey: "dedup.rescore-own-key",/
